        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "refactor(infra): add p-map concurrency limit to ports-inspect enrichment",
        "body": "## Summary\n\n**Problem**: `src/infra/ports-inspect.ts` uses `Promise.all(listeners.map(async fn))` without concurrency control when enriching port listener process details. On systems with many listening ports (up to 65535 theoretical maximum), this spawns concurrent subprocesses equal to the port count — each subprocess resolves a command line, user, and parent PID via system tools.\n\n**Solution**: Replace 2 `Promise.all(x.map(async ...))` calls with `pMap(x, ..., { concurrency: 20 })` — zero new dependencies because p-map v7.0.5 is already a direct dependency. It is used across 7+ other modules in the codebase including media-understanding, cron, gateway server startup, and agent session management.\n\n**What changed**: 2 async enrichment locations in `src/infra/ports-inspect.ts` — `enrichUnixListenerProcessInfo` (resolve Unix command line, user, and parent PID for each listener) and the Windows equivalent in `readWindowsNetstatEntries` (resolve Windows image name and command line for each entry).\n\n**What did NOT change**: All existing behavior and return types preserved at small scale. The inner `Promise.all([resolveUnixCommandLine, resolveUnixUser, resolveUnixParentPid])` (fixed 3-element batch per listener, not a map over input) remains unchanged — only the outer map over the listener list is bounded.\n\n## Root Cause\n\nThe `Promise.all(x.map(async fn))` pattern spawns all async operations simultaneously with no concurrency limit. When `x` is a dynamic list from the OS port table, this creates subprocesses for every port in parallel. On a system with hundreds of listening ports this can overwhelm the process table. p-map solves this by limiting in-flight promises to the configured concurrency (20), queuing remaining items until a slot opens. This is a standard Node.js concurrency pattern used across thousands of open-source projects.\n\n## Real behavior proof\n\n**Behavior addressed**: Unbounded concurrent subprocess spawning in system port inspection\n**Real environment tested**: Linux 6.17.0-35-generic / OpenClaw f72e936f266\n**Exact steps or command run after this patch**: `npx tsc --noEmit && npx vitest run src/infra/ports.test.ts`\n**After-fix evidence**: `TypeScript: No errors found — src/infra/ports.test.ts → Test Files 1 passed (1), Tests 19 passed (19)`\n**Observed result after the fix**: Compilation clean, all 19 existing unit tests still pass. Same pMap concurrency=20 pattern used in 7+ other modules across the codebase.\n**What was not tested**: No integration test with actual high-port-count systems in a real production environment. The concurrency limit is a purely defensive measure — existing tests cover all code paths at small scale. The pMap call sites are exercised indirectly through the existing 19 unit tests. The inner `Promise.all` (fixed 3-element batch for commandLine/user/parentPid) remains unchanged because it is not a map over variable-length input — only the outer listener map is bounded.\n\n## Tests and validation\n\n### Unit tests\n\n```\nsrc/infra/ports.test.ts → Test Files 1 passed (1), Tests 19 passed (19)\n```\n\n### TypeScript\n\n```\nnpx tsc --noEmit → No errors found\n```\n\n## Risk checklist\n\n- [x] This change is backwards compatible\n- [x] This change has been tested with existing configurations\n- [ ] I have updated relevant documentation\n- [ ] Breaking changes (if any) are documented in Summary\n\n- **Merge-risk explanation**: Minimal — no logic change, only I/O concurrency bounding. pMap is a trusted dependency used in 7+ other modules.\n",
        "branch": "fix/scan-20260714-2-pmap-ports-inspect",
